    "content": "For the 2026 tax season, taxpayers can request an automatic extension to file their federal income tax return until October 15, 2026. The request must be filed by April 15, 2026, using Form 4868. Note: An extension grants more time to file, not to pay; estimated taxes owed are still due by April 15, 2026, to avoid penalties.How to Request a 2026 Extension:IRS Free File: Use IRS Free File to submit Form 4868 electronically at no cost.Pay and File: Pay all or part of your estimated taxes via IRS Direct Pay, debit/credit card, or EFTPS and select \"Extension\" as the reason. This avoids the need to file a separate Form 4868.Mail: Mail a paper Form 4868 to the IRS, postmarked by April 15, 2026.Important Considerations:Avoid Penalties: You must pay 90% of your total tax liability by April 15 to avoid estimated payment penalties.Special Circumstances: U.S. citizens living outside the country may receive an automatic two-month extension.Disaster Relief: Taxpayers in specific declared disaster areas, such as those impacted by Winter Storm Fern in Tennessee, may have postponed deadlines. For the 2026 tax season, taxpayers can request an automatic extension to file their federal income tax return until October 15, 2026. The request must be filed by April 15, 2026, using Form 4868. Note: An extension grants more time to file, not to pay; estimated taxes owed are still due by April 15, 2026, to avoid penalties. Form 4868 How to Request a 2026 Extension: IRS Free File: Use IRS Free File to submit Form 4868 electronically at no cost. IRS Free File Pay and File: Pay all or part of your estimated taxes via IRS Direct Pay, debit/credit card, or EFTPS and select \"Extension\" as the reason. This avoids the need to file a separate Form 4868. IRS Direct Pay EFTPS Mail: Mail a paper Form 4868 to the IRS, postmarked by April 15, 2026. Form 4868 Important Considerations: Avoid Penalties: You must pay 90% of your total tax liability by April 15 to avoid estimated payment penalties. Special Circumstances: U.S. citizens living outside the country may receive an automatic two-month extension. Disaster Relief: Taxpayers in specific declared disaster areas, such as those impacted by Winter Storm Fern in Tennessee, may have postponed deadlines. impacted by Winter Storm Fern in Tennessee",
